import { ExplanationCopy } from "../../explanation"
import { ReaderCopy } from "../../reader-article"

pub fn copy -> ReaderCopy = ReaderCopy {
  title: "Traits",
  summary: (explanation ()).summary,
  purpose: [
    "Suppose different kinds of data should be displayed as labels. Writing one unrelated function for each type would make shared code depend on all their names. A trait gives the shared operation one name, label, while allowing a type-specific implementation.",
    "There are three separate pieces: the trait declares what an operation accepts and returns; an instance defines how it works for a type; a call uses that operation on a value. The small example contains all three."
  ],
  exampleTitle: "Give a ticket a label",
  reading: [
    "struct Ticket { number: Int } introduces a ticket type holding one integer. Ticket { number: 42 } creates the ticket used by main.",
    "trait Label<A> declares an operation for a type represented by A. A is a type parameter: a placeholder that will be replaced by a particular type. fn label value: A -> String requires a function taking that type and returning text. It supplies no function body.",
    "instance Label<Ticket> supplies that body for Ticket. Here A has been replaced by Ticket. The implementation reads value.number, converts the integer to text with show, and prefixes it with \"ticket-\".",
    "When the compiler checks label ticket, it knows ticket has type Ticket. It selects Label<Ticket> and checks its implementation exists. The program then calls that label function and prints ticket-42."
  ],
  rules: [
    "The implementation's availability is checked before execution. Implementation evidence means confirmation that an operation required for a type has a matching instance. You do not need to construct a separate evidence object to make the call.",
    "The choice depends on types, not on the runtime value. Ticket { number: 1 } and Ticket { number: 42 } use the same Label<Ticket> instance. An instance is not chosen by inspecting the number.",
    "A trait does not make Ticket inherit from Label or become a subtype. It does not add fields or inherent dot methods. label ticket is an ordinary function call with an additional requirement for a matching trait implementation.",
    "labelOf<A> is the shared caller in this example. where Label<A> means that A must support label. Person has such an instance, so labelOf (Person { name: \"Aki\" }) returns \"Aki\". Ticket also has one, so labelOf can accept a Ticket without changing its own function body."
  ],
  mistakes: [
    "Declaring Label<A> alone does not supply a label implementation. Calling label with a type lacking an instance fails during type checking; it does not wait for runtime.",
    "Do not write ticket.label merely because Label<Ticket> exists. Dot methods belong to impl blocks; trait functions use ordinary application or qualified names such as Label.label."
  ],
  related: [
    "Check the exact declaration syntax and what is allowed in a trait body.",
    "Check how to implement a declared operation for your type.",
    "See how a generic function states that its argument type must support an operation."
  ]
}

pub fn explanation -> ExplanationCopy = ExplanationCopy {
  question: "How can different types share one named operation?",
  summary: "A trait declares a shared operation; an instance supplies its implementation for a particular type. A generic caller can require that operation without knowing each type's representation.",
  reading: "Label<A> declares label: A -> String. Ticket has an integer number; Person has a name. Each instance supplies label for its own type. labelOf<A> calls label without knowing either representation; where Label<A> requires that the supplied type supports this operation. personLabel is \"Aki\", and label ticket in main produces \"ticket-42\".",
  result: "The result is \"ticket-42\". You can define Label for another owned type and reuse a caller constrained by Label<A>. The benefit is a shared contract, not making every label operation a dot method or inspecting values for a runtime class."
}
